2026-09-11T16:00:00.000Z note implemented by hand on the maintainer's instruction — measured small before the route was chosen: one line at the boundary where readOnlyBacklog is already selected, plus two comment sites, one inverted pin and two register rows.
2026-09-11T16:00:00.001Z note the design changed during implementation, on a measurement. "Never mutate the caller's ticket" — which this ticket's own body called the cheap answer — was implemented first and broke NINE tests, six of which pin the real-run mutation as behaviour. Reading those six produced the ruling: on a real run the mutation travels with persistence.writeTicket so the object and the backlog agree; under dry every writer is a no-op so the object would carry a stage, a history entry and a counter set that exist nowhere. The defect is the asymmetry, not the mutation. Narrowed to dry: 2 tests, both of which had to change.
2026-09-11T16:00:00.002Z note decision entry 090 records it, and docs/GLOSSARY.md's Dry run entry gained the clause in the same change — the sentence already claimed a dry run writes nothing, and advancing a stage in memory is doing one of the things rather than reporting it. The code was catching up with the glossary rather than changing it.
2026-09-11T16:00:00.003Z note the pin at engine.test.ts was INVERTED rather than deleted: it asserted that a dry walk CHANGED the caller's ticket, which was the defect pinned as behaviour. It now asserts the opposite, plus a deep check on history, because a serialised comparison cannot see an alias — meta.iterations is the same object RunContext.counters aliases.
